A: Since you're using NUnit, to include contracts for testing your assertions, you can use a combination with some Pex attributes. For example:

```csharp
[PexMethod]
[PexExpectedException(typeof(ArgumentException), ExpectedExceptionReasons.Contains)]
public void PUT_IndexOfContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, [PexPositiveValue] int x)
{
    // Assumptions already implemented and used as Pex method parameters

    // Implementation and Assertion
    // ...

    // Assertion
    Assert.Throws<ArgumentException>(() => arrList.IndexOf(x));
}
```
This will generate a contract that assumes that when you call `IndexOf(int x)` with a specific item, if the list does not contain this item, the method should throw an `ArgumentException` with reason `Contains`.

NOTE
NUnit.Framework.Assert.Throws is being used to assert that a method throws a specific exception. This method is basically wrapping the method invocation inside a try-catch block and if the expected exception is thrown, the method returns normally. If no exception is thrown, or if a different exception is thrown, the method fails.


Prepare your test data carefully: For instance, you don't want to test `IndexOf` or `LastIndexOf` methods with a value not present in the list. Also, when considering the range of `x`, pick a representative value of the scenarios in which your methods should not throw an `ArgumentException`. For example, `x` should be the value of an item which is in the list when the size of the list is more than 0.

